  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 141 PM MDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nLong term starts quiet but then we'll see a good shot of fall\nweather to start the next work week, giving us cooler\ntemperatures and widespread precipitation followed by a dryout\nand warming period into the middle of the week. Sunday morning\nat least starts out relatively quiet and dry as we'll see\nafternoon temperatures warm in the mid 70's to mid 80's for the\nday. But an incoming shortwave along with increasing moisture\nshould help to usher in a few late evening and overnight showers\nor storms prior to the more eventful Monday and Tuesday. The\nwork week then starts out with a surface cold front approaching\nthe region and moving across into Tuesday, while deep upper\nlevel troughing from the Pacific moves in and across the region.\nThis system brings a nice influx of moisture, with PWAT values\napproaching 170-200% of normal. Scattered showers and\nthunderstorms start to overspread the region into the early\nafternoon hours, but guaranteed moisture encompassing most of\nour forecast area occurs during the evening into the overnight\nhours through Tuesday morning. Initial activity could include\nthunderstorms, with favorable lapse rates and marginal \ninstability alongside some stout bulk wind shear for our eastern\nzones. Therefore, a few storms could be on the stronger side, \nbut with how much cloud cover and overspreading precipitation we\nare expecting, this should be few and far between as of this \nwriting. Otherwise, widespread QPF looks both generous and \nfairly plentiful, with most of our CWA seeing a tenth to a \nquarter of an inch, with locally heavier totals in the strongest\ndownpours. Our mountain zones should be seeing around an inch \nof QPF meanwhile, and overnight temperatures into Tuesday \nmorning will be approaching freezing, meaning they may see their\nfirst snow (albeit light) of the season. Behind the cold front,\ntemperatures on Tuesday will sit around 5-8 degrees below \nnormal, giving us highs in the 50's and 60's. And then expect \nchilly mornings through the rest of the forecast period, as low \ntemperatures bottom out in the 40's, possibly even upper 30's, \nparticularly if we can get any overnight clearing to allow for \neven more cooling. We'll be looking at similarly cool \ntemperatures into Wednesday as highs stay in the 60's, to just \naround 70's, with another chance of precipitation as a shortwave\npasses across with deep upper level troughing just to our north\nas we end the long term.",
